3️⃣ Creating Threads in Java

In Java, a thread represents a separate path of execution inside the JVM.

There are 3 main ways to create threads:

1️⃣ Extending Thread class
2️⃣ Implementing Runnable interface
3️⃣ Using Callable with ExecutorService (modern approach)

1️⃣ Extending the Thread Class

This is the most basic way.

Idea

Create a class that extends Thread and override the run() method.

Example
class MyThread extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread is running");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyThread thread = new MyThread();
        thread.start();   // starts new thread
    }
}
Important Point

start() creates a new thread and internally calls run().

If you call:

thread.run();

then no new thread is created (runs in main thread).

Problems with this Approach

Extending Thread is not recommended in real systems because:

Java does not support multiple inheritance

Your class cannot extend any other class

Example problem:

class PaymentService extends Thread

Now it cannot extend another base class.

2️⃣ Implementing Runnable Interface (Preferred)

This is the most commonly used approach.

Idea

Define the task separately using Runnable.

Example
class MyTask implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Task executed by thread");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread thread = new Thread(new MyTask());
        thread.start();
    }
}
Why Runnable is Better

Advantages:

✔ separation of task and thread
✔ allows inheritance from other classes
✔ cleaner design

Example:

class PaymentService implements Runnable

Now it can also extend another class if needed.

Runnable Using Lambda (Java 8)

Cleaner syntax:

Thread thread = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running task");
});

thread.start();
3️⃣ Using Callable (Return Value)

Runnable cannot return a value.

For tasks needing results we use Callable.

Example:

Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    return 10 + 20;
};

But Callable must run through ExecutorService.

4️⃣ Using Executor Framework (Modern Way)

In real backend systems we do not create threads manually.

Instead we use thread pools.

Example:

ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

executor.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task executed by thread pool");
});

Benefits:

✔ thread reuse
✔ better performance
✔ avoids creating thousands of threads

Frameworks like Spring Boot internally use thread pools when handling HTTP requests via servers like Apache Tomcat.

