For GS-level backend understanding, this topic is not just about syntax—it’s about why modern systems avoid manual thread creation.

4️⃣ Problem with Creating Threads Manually

At first glance, creating threads manually looks simple:

for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    new Thread(() -> {
        System.out.println("Processing task");
    }).start();
}

But in real backend systems, this approach causes serious performance and stability issues.

1️⃣ Thread Creation Is Expensive

Creating a thread requires:

allocating memory (stack)

registering with OS scheduler

context setup

Typical memory per thread:

~1 MB stack memory

Example:

1000 threads → ~1 GB memory

This can crash your server.

2️⃣ No Limit on Thread Creation

If every request creates a thread:

User Request → New Thread

Then:

10,000 users → 10,000 threads

Problems:

CPU overload

memory exhaustion

application crash

This is called thread explosion.

3️⃣ Context Switching Overhead

CPU can execute only one thread per core at a time.

Example machine:

8 CPU cores

But application creates:

2000 threads

The OS constantly switches between threads:

Thread1 → Thread2 → Thread3 → Thread4

This is called context switching.

Too much context switching causes:

CPU waste

slower execution

latency spikes

4️⃣ Poor Resource Management

Manual threads have no lifecycle control.

Example:

new Thread(task).start();

Problems:

cannot track threads

difficult to stop them

difficult to manage failures

Large systems require:

monitoring

limits

graceful shutdown

Manual threads do not provide these.

5️⃣ Difficult Error Handling

If a thread crashes:

throw new RuntimeException();

The main application may not even know about it.

Result:

silent failures
lost tasks

In production systems this is dangerous.

6️⃣ No Task Queue

Manual threads execute immediately.

But real systems need task queuing.

Example:

100 tasks arrive
10 workers available

Ideal behavior:

10 tasks execute
90 wait in queue

Manual thread creation cannot handle this efficiently.

7️⃣ Hard to Scale

In microservices or APIs:

1000 requests per second

Manual threads cannot scale because:

uncontrolled thread growth

system instability

8️⃣ Real Backend Example

Imagine a banking service.

User performs payment.

Each request creates a thread:

User1 → Thread1
User2 → Thread2
User3 → Thread3
...

At peak traffic:

50,000 requests

Your server will crash.

Modern servers instead use thread pools, like those used in Apache Tomcat when running applications built with Spring Boot.

9️⃣ The Solution: Thread Pools

Instead of creating threads repeatedly:

Create a fixed number of reusable threads.

Example:

ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

executor.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task executed");
});

Now behavior becomes:

10 threads handle tasks
extra tasks wait in queue
threads reused

Benefits:

controlled concurrency

lower memory usage

better CPU utilization

stability

