6️⃣ Types of Executors

In Java, the Executors utility class provides different types of thread pools.

Main ones:

1️⃣ Fixed Thread Pool
2️⃣ Cached Thread Pool
3️⃣ Single Thread Executor
4️⃣ Scheduled Thread Pool
5️⃣ Work Stealing Pool (Advanced)

1️⃣ Fixed Thread Pool

Creates a fixed number of threads.

Example
ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

Meaning:

5 threads available
tasks run concurrently
extra tasks wait in queue

Example usage:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    executor.submit(() -> {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
    });
}

Execution example:

Thread1 → Task1
Thread2 → Task2
Thread3 → Task3
Thread4 → Task4
Thread5 → Task5
Queue → Task6..Task10
When to Use

Best for:

web servers

backend APIs

database processing

This is most commonly used in production systems.

2️⃣ Cached Thread Pool

Creates threads dynamically as needed.

Example
ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

Behavior:

New task arrives → create thread
Idle thread → reused

Threads that stay idle for 60 seconds are removed.

Example
executor.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println("Processing task");
});
When to Use

Good for:

short-lived tasks

burst traffic

Risk

If too many requests come:

10000 tasks → 10000 threads

Possible memory crash.

3️⃣ Single Thread Executor

Creates only one worker thread.

Example
ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

Execution:

Task1 → executed
Task2 → waits
Task3 → waits

Tasks run sequentially.

Example:

executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task1"));
executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task2"));

Output order:

Task1
Task2
When to Use

Used when order matters.

Example:

event processing

transaction logging

message queues

4️⃣ Scheduled Thread Pool

Used for delayed or periodic tasks.

Example
ScheduledExecutorService scheduler =
        Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(2);

Run task after delay:

scheduler.schedule(() -> {
    System.out.println("Delayed task");
}, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

Run task repeatedly:

scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
    System.out.println("Running job");
}, 0, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
When to Use

Common uses:

cron jobs

cache refresh

retry jobs

monitoring tasks

5️⃣ Work Stealing Pool (Advanced)

Uses ForkJoinPool internally.

Example:

ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newWorkStealingPool();

Concept:

Each thread has its own queue of tasks.

If one thread finishes early:

It steals tasks from another thread

Hence the name work stealing.

When to Use

Good for:

CPU-intensive tasks

parallel algorithms

large data processing

