Spring Boot achieves this using Thread Pools + Async processing.

2️⃣ Enable Async Processing

First step: enable async support.

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig {
}

@EnableAsync tells Spring:

Allow background thread execution
3️⃣ Using @Async

Spring provides the @Async annotation.

Example:

@Service
public class EmailService {

    @Async
    public void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Sending email...");
    }
}

Now when this method is called:

emailService.sendEmail();

Execution happens in a separate thread from the pool.

4️⃣ Default Thread Pool Behavior

If you don't configure anything:

Spring Boot creates a default SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor.

Problems:

Creates new thread for each task
Not ideal for production

So in real systems we configure ThreadPoolTaskExecutor.

5️⃣ Custom Thread Pool Configuration

Example configuration:

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig {

    @Bean(name = "taskExecutor")
    public Executor taskExecutor() {

        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor =
                new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();

        executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(10);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(50);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("AsyncThread-");

        executor.initialize();

        return executor;
    }
}

Meaning:

Core threads → 5
Maximum threads → 10
Queue capacity → 50
6️⃣ How This Thread Pool Works

Suppose:

Core pool size = 5
Max pool size = 10
Queue capacity = 50

Execution flow:

First 5 tasks → run immediately
Next 50 tasks → wait in queue
More tasks → new threads up to 10
Beyond that → rejected
