2️⃣ Race Condition & Thread Safety
What is Race Condition?

When multiple threads access shared data simultaneously, the final result becomes unpredictable.

Example:

balance = 1000

Thread1:

withdraw 500

Thread2:

withdraw 300

Expected result:

200

But possible results:

700
500
200

This is a race condition.

Why It Happens

Threads share heap memory.

Example:

Thread1 reads balance
Thread2 reads balance
Thread1 updates
Thread2 overwrites
Thread Safety

Thread-safe code ensures:

Correct result even with multiple threads

Methods to achieve thread safety:

Synchronization
Locks
Atomic variables
Immutable objects
Concurrent collections
3️⃣ Synchronization & Locks
What is Synchronization?

Synchronization ensures only one thread accesses critical code at a time.

Example:

synchronized void withdraw(int amount) {
    balance -= amount;
}

Meaning:

Only one thread enters this method
Synchronized Block

Instead of locking entire method:

synchronized(this) {
   balance -= amount;
}

Better control over locking.

Problems with Synchronization

Too much synchronization causes:

thread blocking
performance degradation
ReentrantLock (Advanced Lock)

Java provides explicit locks.

Example:

Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

lock.lock();
try {
   balance -= amount;
} finally {
   lock.unlock();
}

Advantages:

better control
tryLock()
timeout support
fair locking
