1️⃣ Deadlock (Most Asked Concurrency Question)
What is Deadlock?

A deadlock occurs when two or more threads wait forever for resources held by each other.

Example:

Thread1 holds LockA → waiting for LockB
Thread2 holds LockB → waiting for LockA

Both threads wait forever.

Simple Example
class DeadlockExample {

    private static final Object lock1 = new Object();
    private static final Object lock2 = new Object();

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock1) {

                try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (Exception e) {}

                synchronized (lock2) {
                    System.out.println("Thread1 acquired both locks");
                }
            }
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (lock2) {

                try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (Exception e) {}

                synchronized (lock1) {
                    System.out.println("Thread2 acquired both locks");
                }
            }
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}

Possible execution:

Thread1 → lock1
Thread2 → lock2
Thread1 → waiting for lock2
Thread2 → waiting for lock1

System freezes.

Four Conditions for Deadlock

Deadlock occurs only if these 4 conditions exist:

1️⃣ Mutual Exclusion
2️⃣ Hold and Wait
3️⃣ No Preemption
4️⃣ Circular Wait

Remove any one condition → deadlock prevented.

How to Prevent Deadlocks
1️⃣ Lock Ordering

Always acquire locks in the same order.

Always lock A → then B
Never B → then A
2️⃣ TryLock

Using timeout-based locking.

lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

Thread stops waiting forever.

3️⃣ Avoid Nested Locks

Reduce complexity of locking.

Interview Answer

If interviewer asks:

What is deadlock and how do you prevent it?

Strong answer:

Deadlock occurs when multiple threads wait indefinitely for resources held by each other due to circular dependency. It can be prevented by techniques like consistent lock ordering, timeout-based locking using tryLock, avoiding nested locks, and reducing shared resources.

